se_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/9ac3f7232aace7e5cf729d6c24b833e16346e24b02e413ce4b683c960b1ba2cb.png)\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/792a75b546453e3a3c47669fc7ec65435534b82faeba04069c1314ae8d16ac41.png)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/0da0a391dd9232585c5cdb6de3ba6c90f54d042451e738d535d5641e4b5d1d42.png) as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/ff223d6ebb226a47ef2ce7ddd6f31ba1d4423a09b234fd9527699bd0ae2c02a6.png) these solutions approach the origin as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/936d454a72dcd557a879b9269c9f72018420e7a956cc3050c134f04aa9cf66b9.png)\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/ff535f17581c5a93045c3c23b8b33ca2996be6dfca63ed7808bc5fec809d2377.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/bf2bfecbb87c3af5c4ea1011465079f66abf2f345f5f2675eb8bef56e6894465.png) has the equation \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/1e837e3cd9e4ebb7ddb86da37b0d0cbfe3d80d3ed912ff20e63d433f3067f42f.png)\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/0fd6486776de0b9d85f1259ba9596f452f36d7f83d413e8722d78ed86f80434d.png)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/3e1a064e45c38a2428e7502496465510c44bacf80eb8177afc26edf4dd3ae406.png) as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/ff223d6ebb226a47ef2ce7ddd6f31ba1d4423a09b234fd9527699bd0ae2c02a6.png) these solutions approach the origin as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/c1cd949d7f80c2b73b9edd4582392f925b011245d2510efdb25699b86b40a8f6.png)\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/317fe507f591931ea371db43b78a83b1741c427ef89dd90571f0a0030ed70c20.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/df829bce7d36af7933a45ecf7982bbd07b12e21d140bd619bccf5f90abebb8f1.png)\nthus, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a784f008958aa2ecc262a1ef4232508ab283c70262655f6ae2747f158685a48b.png) is the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue).\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/fa359c604ba4bb24805097dd63e07783f72bba9c3e2657ec6c29a3fee129e3e6.png) solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/ec6e981dc2ac04d82a79e2b13a53b8d54364d155310cbe38bf3724792b89047d.png) as they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/3a6d8a97c6d07c78562c73dbd1abef1b1a3c0d9f38cd14f4f9874df091492b9e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166475 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "1c7567cf-f861-5088-b43e-ff94fcff0c91" :question/math-academy-id "e-21634" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 2]^T,$ respectively. Complete the following statements about the system by filling in the blanks.\n\nIdentify stable and unstable lines, and classify the equilibrium point (origin)." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/1c9e5558e6c3c4a97881100ad5a36c5ab6b2c602eb521123371bdde9c8450a6e.png)\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a14a160428831f0fdf8a22ebcb932e655046fe4c4e258bc0d83b25c97ecfafbb.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -\\dfrac12x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/c2114a7edccbf2b296cca7e1cb9c08cd29ba063745a00d55de2ed06026aa2060.png)\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-\\dfrac12x$ is an unstable line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/01186c121850d622e0f4216bb6375f7ef11d2d4cef0dd2acf2eb8f99d2a16643.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/0a96347bbf236e77b719686bfe2cb275ad9a4cc1061edc1dacf3a70be423d9fc.png)\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-2x$ is a stable line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is a saddle point. \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/2710e11309547500aa3e9ba89f31281cbbc28244509046ed25a6c151c10c23ed.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166476 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "986f9190-9e73-5e08-b08d-ec779933b0cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21635" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/270b7c6ff796937603596f26e70886f41438ce87c043d3a1b4fc221398b8692f.png)\n\n \n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T,$ respectively. \n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1,$ $\\lambda_2,$ and $0.$" :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/7282a63a66c9f449844292764e4e7b6f1ec472a47ff18aa7fd0c68dbe6042dbe.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/9bff79e13c06645253e57b8e6f43689c7ce21ac818c3ffdc677e03b5a9b08eb7.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/d241239c6475fbcf9559c2fcbbb8ca2805a6692fd1a9b3966fe8b38441075a4d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/9804740137aa14411a15e6a8fce4d495527bdc1980dbd0cda294cfcf05e89dd8.png)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 >  0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/b06ebf2f3fa8f0616092292e746e84c7e925c42d38cf1c31dc4f6edede6d001b.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 0,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/fb40117d63e76b2b2db59313b173b04a9a23ef2a28493a5041584e4c7c60c67d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/44c8b9978f9c0db5fc39702755042f1887a95fb2034bb6b01dd8d025b589aba5.png)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 > 0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_2.$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda_2 > \\lambda_1 > 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ada3a84a-f979-5aee-9422-7acb8e430c11" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8287e37-8236-596b-ad50-20c0ef402e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361880} {:answer/id #uuid "78712ffb-cad7-54e9-9716-bccbd6372770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361881} {:answer/id #uuid "61f681f3-39db-59e7-a54b-d37f85dba75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361882} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3ce017-370a-5c35-92a4-19744bbd64dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61f681f3-39db-59e7-a54b-d37f85dba75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361882} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b9c86d7-cb6b-5dcb-9819-86104279a649" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee32541-8a79-50ef-918d-7f8d75fe1ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361885} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc4c6ff-da6f-5635-9dff-76f2418d65df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361886} {:answer/id #uuid "c85b5e06-9610-548d-a4d4-35a77214454e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361887} {:answer/id #uuid "86c768a7-ba16-5a83-b3de-463289f7ede1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcc4c6ff-da6