 field" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5ad69d8-de20-5ad8-9a81-6b2a68f34487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "phase portrait" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361916} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a1aa9c-6b39-585a-9542-83855f47107a" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe606e21-16dc-5275-bda3-617803b9b0bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332833" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/f1335e5420fe6e475cded1092e9b1ba621e0d88f46110b69ffef5e35ed080d6e.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a53bb4e6cd7552b92424b168d0cda90fa1085b5e72fbf8a3c37a3a948bdcf9ad.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}}  of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/ecccffb89d43b8de4ce0952d9b8d9686e74670b4d86b29687c76355b70a6ac7d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/f1335e5420fe6e475cded1092e9b1ba621e0d88f46110b69ffef5e35ed080d6e.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a53bb4e6cd7552b92424b168d0cda90fa1085b5e72fbf8a3c37a3a948bdcf9ad.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/923e79b665f4529e579776188731896e6ce301743394e2102132f2cee7f6eaa9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa7a9293-1ec2-57fa-b070-9f504779cc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361920} {:answer/id #uuid "c3faa657-c20f-5778-a495-6760b141039e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361921} {:answer/id #uuid "36de76f3-a03a-5bf9-9cc1-a80ba7251124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361922} {:answer/id #uuid "5d847263-7e1b-5f6f-81a7-042c6fa8f877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa7a9293-1ec2-57fa-b070-9f504779cc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361920} :answer-field/id #uuid "378fadba-9e44-54ee-8fff-04ee6c162576" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361919} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2562ddd9-d3c3-5e0d-92f5-064848dc6108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "toward the origin" :db/id #edb/re