lue "infinity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361943} {:answer/id #uuid "46d037e1-395a-5132-8bfc-eacbf81339c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46d037e1-395a-5132-8bfc-eacbf81339c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361944} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7f682a1-a7f9-58ec-90f4-07556c282794" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361942} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a846b5f-1cf4-5a32-8bf1-9ca5325989e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361946} {:answer/id #uuid "26bfacda-734b-5c7e-a45f-28b547428e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361947} {:answer/id #uuid "baeaa417-9b73-52ee-9aa0-ad77e64dc5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baeaa417-9b73-52ee-9aa0-ad77e64dc5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361948} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a727a4-2138-5f4a-9799-d44331e5cf42" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "179f3ace-36b9-5ae3-b795-98edf695f28e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333041" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/56a90f9fb61640cc2eeca8e2f21f69381a1d53d008701ec457ade0fed7e54a64.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}." :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/1f166fad96cd30dbc0b9cf05fdabcccd26cd68e25fc26b91ae357d4015813614.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/cbb8268b959a09af3d0b57573ff08f45a2fb87f376d87f284df49cd5094412d9.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ada3a84a-f979-5aee-9422-7acb8e430c11" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a335f46e-ba05-5ce6-866f-3882d14f0e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361951} {:answer/id #uuid "3737205d-6266-58e9-a0fe-c6fc8d301db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361952} {:answer/id #uuid "4de2ac93-dfcd-5f38-9307-6b53aa79738f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361953} {:answer/id #uuid "ec98af45-0f67-5922-a2ce-2226adbe944a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec98af45-0f67-5922-a2ce-2226adbe944a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361954} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8e584e-d67f-511e-90a6-d46adfe6de9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361950} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61c98105-4315-56e3-8dfa-e045ee5b99d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/i