wer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6aaaa14-7be9-5a90-b644-c6d13a1b873c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362027} {:answer/id #uuid "26c66de7-2b16-590b-bbc6-ed0bd5c852d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362028} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5ed763-67c4-5a26-9d71-955b65c1d62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc5ed763-67c4-5a26-9d71-955b65c1d62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362029} :answer-field/id #uuid "21df9ef7-fd64-57ea-97cf-ea605ec1b116" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362026} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b197dca5-9c5f-5136-90e2-8d1128e04f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362031} {:answer/id #uuid "38de91e3-dad7-54ae-85fd-9aa82b69cc4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38de91e3-dad7-54ae-85fd-9aa82b69cc4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362032} :answer-field/id #uuid "10aed614-c17f-588e-9ef9-0ce78a43dd6e" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8cf237e-ffc7-539d-8c10-75ca847f73ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-333328" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$" :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/c675f557abede7c8f21eadf3fd65dbd522c5ee9ad0a4ed0a804b03f357c1dc3b.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a25c57a021e42b2fa78b71f9ab79f0676a16f7c4f1082af0e9502d4d63ed4e46.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are negative. The eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is the one closest to $0.$\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{2}$ as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{1}<{\\lambda }_{2}<0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c77329a-553b-5136-90ca-3332a11fab58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362035} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdc1dd6-0ea9-5d8f-a55b-1af1493d2930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362036}