e point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362063} {:answer/id #uuid "741b18ba-c2d1-5870-920a-aeacfe95e609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "741b18ba-c2d1-5870-920a-aeacfe95e609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362064} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02d5dd2-b009-5eea-b7e1-156bf47e9eb0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d111108f-9aed-5eb7-8811-2065601ee640" :question/math-academy-id "q-333640" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$\n$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/0d9e032bfffa68f487347d6c169999e313247e89ef7de3fae28edb216dcf718f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d9e517d-863d-5801-b4bc-23f019691fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362067} {:answer/id #uuid "e65349ea-a50f-538e-8cae-b4173969c611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e65349ea-a50f-538e-8cae-b4173969c611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362068} :answer-field/id #uuid "40274f6b-e58d-578f-8bb3-78c74530963b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362066} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95f7264f-2b4b-5294-b6af-32feff1a559a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362070} {:answer/id #uuid "1b11aba9-1512-59b2-9c9c-e117f5fa0ad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362071} {:ans